WHAT: The cycle-2 rewrite's new third allowance — an *ast.Ident that resolves (via Obj identity) to a CapabilityID-typed parameter of the enclosing function/function-literal, "forwarded verbatim" — has no interprocedural or dataflow verification behind it. Two concrete, independently confirmed bypass shapes follow directly from this:

(1) WRAPPER-FUNCTION LAUNDERING: capabilitylint only pattern-matches call expressions whose callee name is literally "DefineCapability"/"MustDefineCapability" (capabilityConstructorName). A user-defined wrapper function around either — exactly the shape MustDefineCapability's own required implementation is, and exactly the shape rule 2 was added this cycle to stop flagging as a false positive — is itself completely invisible to the analyzer at its OWN call sites, because a call to the wrapper is not a call to DefineCapability/MustDefineCapability by name. Nothing ever verifies that the wrapper's callers supply a canonical constant.

(2) PARAMETER REBINDING (direct and via closure capture): rule 2 checks only that the *ast.Ident's Obj matches a CapabilityID-typed parameter's Obj — it performs no data-flow analysis of whether that parameter's value was reassigned before being forwarded. Since a parameter is an ordinary local variable, a plain `id = CapabilityID("literal")` reassignment (same Obj, new value) inside the function body, followed by forwarding `id` to Define/MustDefineCapability, is accepted with zero findings. The same reassignment inside a closure that captures the parameter is equally invisible.

EMPIRICALLY CONFIRMED (scratch _test.go added temporarily to internal/codegen/capabilitylint, then deleted — worktree left clean, verified via `git status --porcelain`):

  func wrapDefine(id CapabilityID, ...) (Capability, error) { return DefineCapability(id, ...) }
  func evil() { wrapDefine(CapabilityID("raw.literal.laundered"), ...) }
  -> 0 findings anywhere in the file (the wrapDefine body's forwarding is correctly allowed by rule 2; the evil() call site passing a raw literal to wrapDefine is never inspected at all, since wrapDefine is not a recognized constructor name).

  func f(id CapabilityID) {
      id = CapabilityID("raw.literal.rebound")
      MustDefineCapability(id, ...)
  }
  -> 0 findings (id still resolves to the parameter's Obj at the call site, even though its value was reassigned to a literal one line earlier).

  func outer(id CapabilityID) func() {
      return func() {
          id = CapabilityID("raw.literal.closure.rebound")
          MustDefineCapability(id, ...)
      }
  }
  -> 0 findings (same mechanism, via a captured-and-reassigned closure variable).

  For contrast, verbatim closure capture WITHOUT reassignment (the actually-intended-safe shape) is correctly still allowed (0 findings, expected) — confirming the bypass is specifically about the missing reassignment/dataflow check, not a general closure-handling gap.

